[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to InOrder Verification in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing unit tests in Java, verifying that methods are called on mocks is crucial. However, sometimes the exact sequence or order in which these methods are executed matters just as much as whether they were called at all. Mockito provides the InOrder utility to verify the relative execution order of method calls."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use InOrder Verification?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensures state transitions happen in the correct sequence (e.g., validate before save, then notify)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Helps debug complex workflows where method execution order dictates business logic validity."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prevents race conditions or logical bugs caused by out-of-order dependency interactions."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use InOrder verification, you first need to create an instance of InOrder using Mockito.inOrder(), passing in the mock objects you want to monitor."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"InOrder inOrder = Mockito.inOrder(firstMock, secondMock);"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the InOrder object is created, you use it to verify interactions in the exact sequence they are expected to occur."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"inOrder.verify(firstMock).doSomethingFirst();
inOrder.verify(secondMock).doSomethingSecond();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Complete Working Example"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is a complete example demonstrating how to test a service that interacts with multiple dependencies in a strict sequence."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@Test
public void testExecutionOrder() {
    // Given
    Validator validator = mock(Validator.class);
    Database db = mock(Database.class);
    NotificationService notifier = mock(NotificationService.class);

    OrderService service = new OrderService(validator, db, notifier);

    // When
    service.processOrder("order123");

    // Then
    InOrder inOrder = inOrder(validator, db, notifier);
    inOrder.verify(validator).validate("order123");
    inOrder.verify(db).save("order123");
    inOrder.verify(notifier).sendConfirmation("order123");
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Important Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not include every single interaction in the InOrder block; only verify the sequence that matters to the test."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"InOrder does not require checking all method calls, but it will fail if the verified methods occur out of the specified sequence."}]
